discovery, dating, material, and iconography.

---

### 1. Archaeology Magazine – “World of the Griffin Warrior” (Archaeological 
Institute of America)

- **Title:** World of the Griffin Warrior  
- **URL:** 
https://archaeology.org/issues/september-october-2019/features/greece-pylos-myce
naean-warrior-grave/  
- **Publisher/Author:** Archaeology Magazine (Archaeological Institute of 
America); article by Eric A. Powell  
- **Date:** September/October 2019 issue (exact day not specified; magazine 
issue date 2019)  
- **Source type:** Academic/heritage magazine (peer-reviewed content, published 
by a major archaeological institution)  
- **Query that triggered it:** "Ring of Nestor Mycenaean gold ring 
archaeological artifact discovery site dating iconography 
-site:en.wikipedia.org"  
- **Relevance:** High. Directly discusses the gold signet rings found in a grave
near the Palace of Nestor, their discovery context, approximate date, material, 
and iconography. This grave is adjacent to the Mycenaean “Palace of Nestor,” and
the rings are a key set of artifacts likely to be meant by “Ring of Nestor” in 
later popular usage.

- **Content / faithful summary:**
  - The article describes the discovery, in 2015, of an undisturbed Mycenaean 
warrior’s grave close to the Palace of Nestor at Pylos, on the southwestern 
Peloponnese.
  - The grave is popularly known as the “Griffin Warrior” tomb because of an 
ivory plaque depicting a griffin found among the grave goods.
  - Archaeologists Jack L. Davis and Sharon R. Stocker (University of 
Cincinnati) led the excavation.
  - The burial is dated to around 1500 BCE (Late Helladic I period). The dating 
is based on stylistic analysis of the grave goods and their relationship to 
Minoan and early Mycenaean material culture.
  - The grave contained more than 2,000 artifacts, including bronze weapons, 
jewelry, sealstones, and gold signet rings.
  - The article notes that the four gold signet rings are among the most 
important finds. They bear elaborate engraved scenes rendered in Minoan style, 
demonstrating strong cultural connections between Crete and mainland Mycenaeans.
  - Iconography on the rings includes scenes of human figures, animals, and 
mythologically charged motifs; one ring shows a bull-jumping scene, closely 
paralleled in Minoan palace art from Crete.
  - The rings are described as solid gold signet rings whose faces (bezels) 
carry glyptic engravings in miniature detail. They were found alongside other 
items of gold jewelry in the grave, emphasizing the wealth and elite status of 
the buried individual.
  - The discovery context is clearly near the Palace of Nestor, but the article 
treats the grave and its artifacts as slightly earlier than the palace itself, 
which dates to a later phase of Mycenaean occupation (ca. 13th century BCE).

