[tools:8b8e8565-8c09-628b-d4be-7bfb4d4698b9] [AIMessage] Below is a structured 
set of sources and extracted material about the “Ring of Nestor” context — i.e.,
the Mycenaean gold signet rings from the Griffin Warrior grave near the Palace 
of Nestor at Pylos. I have focused on institutional / academic / heritage 
sources and on details of discovery, dating, material, and iconography.

---

### 1. Archaeology Magazine – “World of the Griffin Warrior” (Archaeological 
Institute of America)

- **Title:** World of the Griffin Warrior  
- **URL:** 
https://archaeology.org/issues/september-october-2019/features/greece-pylos-myce
naean-warrior-grave/  
- **Publisher/Author:** Archaeology Magazine (Archaeological Institute of 
America); article by Eric A. Powell  
- **Date:** September/October 2019 issue (exact day not specified; magazine 
issue date 2019)  
- **Source type:** Academic/heritage magazine (peer-reviewed content, published 
by a major archaeological institution)  
- **Query that triggered it:** "Ring of Nestor Mycenaean gold ring 
archaeological artifact discovery site dating iconography 
-site:en.wikipedia.org"  
- **Relevance:** High. Directly discusses the gold signet rings found in a grave
near the Palace of Nestor, their discovery context, approximate date, material, 
and iconography. This grave is adjacent to the Mycenaean “Palace of Nestor,” and
the rings are a key set of artifacts likely to be meant by “Ring of Nestor” in 
later popular usage.

- **Content / faithful summary:**
  - The article describes the discovery, in 2015, of an undisturbed Mycenaean 
warrior’s grave close to the Palace of Nestor at Pylos, on the southwestern 
Peloponnese.
  - The grave is popularly known as the “Griffin Warrior” tomb because of an 
ivory plaque depicting a griffin found among the grave goods.
  - Archaeologists Jack L. Davis and Sharon R. Stocker (University of 
Cincinnati) led the excavation.
  - The burial is dated to around 1500 BCE (Late Helladic I period). The dating 
is based on stylistic analysis of the grave goods and their relationship to 
Minoan and early Mycenaean material culture.
  - The grave contained more than 2,000 artifacts, including bronze weapons, 
jewelry, sealstones, and gold signet rings.
  - The article notes that the four gold signet rings are among the most 
important finds. They bear elaborate engraved scenes rendered in Minoan style, 
demonstrating strong cultural connections between Crete and mainland Mycenaeans.
  - Iconography on the rings includes scenes of human figures, animals, and 
mythologically charged motifs; one ring shows a bull-jumping scene, closely 
paralleled in Minoan palace art from Crete.
  - The rings are described as solid gold signet rings whose faces (bezels) 
carry glyptic engravings in miniature detail. They were found alongside other 
items of gold jewelry in the grave, emphasizing the wealth and elite status of 
the buried individual.
  - The discovery context is clearly near the Palace of Nestor, but the article 
treats the grave and its artifacts as slightly earlier than the palace itself, 
which dates to a later phase of Mycenaean occupation (ca. 13th century BCE).

---

### 2. University of Cincinnati – “Lord of the rings” (UC Magazine feature)

- **Title:** Lord of the rings  
- **URL:** 
https://magazine.uc.edu/editors_picks/recent_features/lordoftherings.html  
- **Publisher/Author:** University of Cincinnati Magazine; authorship attributed
to university communications staff (often John Bach or similar; exact name needs
to be checked in page text)  
- **Date:** October 3, 2016  
- **Source type:** University / institutional news feature  
- **Query that triggered it:** "Ring of Nestor Mycenaean gold ring 
archaeological artifact discovery site dating iconography 
-site:en.wikipedia.org"  
- **Relevance:** High. Institutional account by the excavation team’s 
university; provides discovery circumstances, dating, material (gold), and 
detailed description of imagery on the rings.

- **Content / faithful summary:**
  - The article reports on the discovery in 2015 of the “Griffin Warrior” grave 
near the Palace of Nestor at Pylos by UC archaeologists Jack L. Davis and Sharon
R. Stocker.
  - It focuses on four gold signet rings found in the grave, emphasizing their 
exceptional craftsmanship and iconography.
  - Dating:
    - The grave is dated to the Late Helladic I period, around 1500 BCE, based 
on typology and stylistic comparison of the grave goods to Aegean Bronze Age 
artifacts.
    - The article notes that this is early in the Mycenaean era and roughly 
contemporary with the zenith of Minoan civilization on Crete.
  - Material:
    - The rings are solid gold, described as “gold signet rings” or “gold seal 
rings.”
    - Their faces are engraved with minute scenes in relief, using techniques 
typical of Minoan glyptic art.
  - Iconography / motifs:
    - One ring depicts a bull-leaping scene, with a human figure vaulting over a
bull, echoing imagery known from Minoan frescoes and sealstones.
    - Another ring shows a female figure, possibly a goddess or priestess, 
standing in a ritual context, with other figures and symbols around her.
    - Additional scenes include animals and complex ritual settings; the article
emphasizes the “Minoan” style and iconography, suggesting that the rings were 
either produced on Crete or strongly influenced by Cretan art.
  - Discovery context:
    - The grave lies just outside the remains of the Mycenaean Palace of Nestor.
The article explains that the palace, known from Homeric tradition, dates to a 
later phase, but the grave predates it by several centuries.
    - The burial was intact and rich, indicating the high status of the deceased
and offering rare insight into early Mycenaean elite culture.
  - Significance:
    - UC archaeologists stress that the rings provide some of the best evidence 
for Mycenaean adoption and reinterpretation of Minoan religious and mythological
imagery.
    - The feature links the rings to broader questions about the rise of 
Mycenaean power and its relationship to Crete.

