[AIMessage → tool] create_analysis({'text': "The available evidence indicates 
that the name 'Ring of Nestor' can refer to at least two distinct artifacts in 
scholarly and popular discourse: (1) a solid-gold signet or seal ring of Late 
Minoan date from a shaft grave at Pylos, associated with the Mycenaean palace 
complex often called the Palace of Nestor, and (2) a gold signet ring reportedly
found on the island of Ithaca in 1925 and discussed by Arthur Evans in 
connection with Homeric traditions about King Nestor. Modern reports on the 
Griffin Warrior tomb near the Palace of Nestor describe four gold signet rings 
with Minoan-style iconography, one of which some media and secondary sources 
label the 'Ring of Nestor'. Meanwhile, Evans and subsequent Aegean prehistorians
use the name for an earlier-discovered ring, frequently linked in scholarship to
Homeric Nestor and to debates about the historicity of Homeric geography. For 
the purposes of a Wikipedia-style article with clear scope, it is appropriate to
foreground the better-documented Pylos/Griffin Warrior context while 
acknowledging in a dedicated section that the epithet has also been applied to a
different Ithaca ring, clarifying similarities and differences. Care is needed 
to avoid conflating the two objects or implying that there is a single canonical
'Ring of Nestor' in archaeological literature.", 'query_id': 
'20260627174245146781_a9f79254'})
