{"accepted": true, "revision_requested": true, "severity": "soft", "report": "",
"salvaged_report": "# Ring of Nestor\n\n## Lead\n\nThe Ring of Nestor is the 
name given to a gold signet ring and related Late Bronze Age seal material 
discussed by Sir Arthur J. Evans in his 1925 monograph The ring of Nestor: A 
glimpse into the Minoan after-world, which examines a sepulchral assemblage of 
gold signet rings and bead seals from a tomb at Thisbê in Boeotia, Greece. Evans
placed the ring within the Minoan–Mycenaean cultural and chronological framework
and interpreted its engraved imagery in connection with Minoan conceptions of 
the afterlife. In later scholarship, the expression “Ring of Nestor” has also 
been used in comparative discussions of other Mycenaean gold signet rings, 
including pieces from a tholos tomb at Pylos and the four solid-gold seal rings 
from the Griffin Warrior tomb near the Palace of Nestor, but these artifacts 
form distinct finds with different archaeological contexts and should not be 
conflated. [7][9][10][1][2][3][4][5][6]\n\n## Discovery and 
description\n\nArthur J. Evans’s study The ring of Nestor was published in 
London in 1925 and focuses on what he identified as a gold signet ring, together
with a group of associated gold signet rings and bead seals, from a sepulchral 
context at Thisbê in Boeotia. Library catalogues and digital editions describe 
the work as an archaeological monograph on a specific named ring and its 
accompanying treasure from a tomb, with the Ring of Nestor treated as the 
central artifact. [7][9][8]\n\nEvans described the Ring of Nestor as a gold 
signet ring with a comparatively large oval bezel used as a sealing surface, 
attached to a hoop forming the ring. The publication includes photographic 
plates illustrating the ring’s physical appearance and seal design, situating it
within a broader assemblage of gold signet rings and bead seals from the same 
tomb. [7]\n\nThe book is structured in two main parts, the first dealing with 
the sepulchral treasure of gold signet rings and bead seals from Thisbê, and the
second offering what Evans termed a glimpse into the Minoan after-world, in 
which the Ring of Nestor’s imagery plays a central role. Digital library records
assign persistent identifiers such as DOI 10.11588/diglit.808 and a URN to the 
work, and reprint editions in English of approximately 96 pages with plates 
attest to its status as a recognized archaeological study. [7][9][8]\n\n## 
Archaeological context\n\nIn his account, Evans situates the Ring of Nestor and 
the associated treasure of gold signet rings and bead seals within a tomb at 
Thisbê in Boeotia, treating the assemblage as a sepulchral deposit with 
religious significance. The work’s title and library subject keywords link the 
ring and its context to Minoan culture, Boeotia, and sites such as Thísvi and 
Kakóvatos, indicating that Evans considered the material part of the wider 
Minoan–Mycenaean world. [7][9]\n\nEvans assigned the Ring of Nestor to a Late 
Bronze Age horizon, specifically to a late Minoan phase within the 
Minoan–Mycenaean chronological framework. His dating places the ring within the 
period when Minoan and Mycenaean cultures interacted and when elaborate gold 
signet rings were used by elites as both personal adornment and sealing devices.
[7]\n\nLater comparative discussions of gold rings sometimes refer to a “Ring of
Nestor” in connection with Mycenaean finds at Pylos, including a gold ring 
discovered in the ashlar cist of Tholos Tomb IV by Lord William Taylour and the 
gold seal rings from the Griffin Warrior tomb near the Palace of Nestor. An 
article on the Griffin Warrior tomb describes that grave as an undisturbed 
Mycenaean warrior burial near the Palace of Nestor at Pylos, dated to the early 
Mycenaean period (Late Helladic I) around the mid‑16th century BCE. 
[10][1][2][3]\n\nArchaeological and institutional publications on the Griffin 
Warrior burial report that the tomb contained a very rich assemblage of grave 
goods, numbering approximately 1,800–2,000 items, including bronze weapons, 
jewelry, carved stones, and four solid‑gold signet or seal rings. These rings, 
sometimes compared to the Ring of Nestor in thematic studies of gold signet 
rings, belong to a separate Mycenaean funerary context and are dated to Late 
Helladic I rather than to the late Minoan phase proposed by Evans for the Thisbê
ring. [7][1][3][2]\n\n## Iconography and interpretations\n\nEvans interpreted 
the engraved imagery on the Ring of Nestor and accompanying gold signet rings 
and bead seals from the Thisbê tomb as reflecting aspects of Minoan beliefs 
about the afterlife. The second part of his monograph, titled \"A glimpse into 
the Minoan after-world\", analyses the seal designs as a visual corpus connected
with funerary religion and eschatology, arguing that the Ring of Nestor offers 
insight into these conceptions. [7][9]\n\nHathiTrust’s description of Evans’s 
work characterizes the Ring of Nestor and the associated gold signet rings and 
bead seals as bearing imagery with religious significance, indicating that later
cataloging of the monograph follows Evans’s emphasis on the seals’ ritual and 
symbolic content. [9]\n\nIn more recent scholarship on Mycenaean gold rings, the
term “Ring of Nestor” appears in comparative discussions that also mention the 
so‑called \"Ring of Minos\" and a gold ring found by Taylour in the ashlar cist 
of Tholos Tomb IV at Pylos, highlighting perceived similarities in iconography 
and prestige between these notable signet rings. Such studies situate the Ring 
of Nestor among a broader group of elite rings whose imagery is used to explore 
Minoan religious and mythological themes and their transmission into Mycenaean 
contexts. [10][6]\n\nThe four gold signet rings from the Griffin Warrior tomb 
near the Palace of Nestor at Pylos, while sometimes discussed alongside the Ring
of Nestor, have distinct iconography. Their engraved scenes include human 
figures, animals, and mythic or ritual compositions, notably a Cretan‑style 
bull‑leaping scene with a figure vaulting over a bull and a scene featuring a 
standing woman in a ritual context. These images are commonly interpreted as 
evidence for strong Minoan influence on the visual culture of early Mycenaean 
elites, but specific identifications of figures as deities or priestesses remain
hypotheses rather than established facts. [1][2][3][4]\n\nA scientific report on
the Griffin Warrior rings draws attention to another gold ring from that tomb 
depicting two bulls flanked by bundles of grain heads, described as a rare 
combination in known Mycenaean imagery and tentatively associated with 
agricultural fertility or authority. Such interpretations underscore the broader
scholarly tendency to read the iconography of Mycenaean gold rings, including 
the Ring of Nestor, in relation to ritual, power, and concepts of the afterlife,
while acknowledging that the symbolism cannot be fully reconstructed from the 
available evidence. [5][7][9][6]\n\n## Name and related artifacts\n\nEvans’s 
monograph explicitly places the phrase \"The ring of Nestor\" in quotation marks
in its title, indicating that he was assigning a specific name to a particular 
gold signet ring from the Thisbê tomb and using that name as the organizing 
concept for his study. Library records echo this phrasing and treat \"The ring 
of Nestor\" as the proper title of the work, reinforcing the association between
the name and the artifact discussed there. [7][9][8]\n\nDigital catalogues 
describe Evans’s book as dealing with a gold signet ring called the Ring of 
Nestor and with other gold signet rings and bead seals from the same tomb, 
emphasizing that the name refers to a specific ring within a larger sepulchral 
treasure rather than to all gold rings of the Late Bronze Age. Subject keywords 
such as \"Nestor\", \"Ring\", \"Minoan culture\", \"Thísvi\", and \"Kakóvatos\" 
link the named artifact to both literary and archaeological frames of reference.
[7][9]\n\nIn later discussions of Mycenaean gold rings, scholars have compared 
the Ring of Nestor with other named rings such as the \"Ring of Minos\", as well
as with a gold ring from the ashlar cist of Tholos Tomb IV at Pylos. One modern 
study on the Griffin Warrior of Pylos notes that an earlier writer may have been
thinking of the gold ring discovered by Taylour in Tholos Tomb IV when referring
to the Ring of Nestor, illustrating that the label has sometimes been applied or
evoked in relation to different artifacts. [10]\n\nThe four gold signet rings 
from the Griffin Warrior tomb near the Palace of Nestor at Pylos have attracted 
popular and scholarly attention and are occasionally discussed under headings 
that evoke Nestor or \"gold rings\" in general. However, institutional and 
scholarly accounts treat these rings as a distinct group: they are solid‑gold 
signet or seal rings with Minoan‑style engravings, found in a single early 
Mycenaean warrior grave and dated to Late Helladic I, and they are not 
themselves identified as the Ring of Nestor in the technical literature. 
[1][2][3][4][6]\n\n## Research history\n\nThe earliest substantial study 
explicitly devoted to the Ring of Nestor is Arthur J. Evans’s 1925 monograph, 
which presents the ring from the Thisbê tomb together with associated gold 
signet rings and bead seals and offers a detailed interpretation of their 
iconography within a Minoan afterlife framework. Major library catalogues record
the work under Evans’s name and provide publication details and identifiers, 
showing that it has been integrated into the bibliographic record of 
archaeological scholarship. [7][9][8]\n\nSubsequent research on Aegean Bronze 
Age gold rings has revisited Evans’s interpretations in light of new finds and 
changing views on Minoan and Mycenaean religion. A recent study on the Griffin 
Warrior of Pylos refers to the Ring of Nestor and the Ring of Minos when 
considering how earlier scholars understood notable gold rings, and compares 
these named artifacts with a ring found by Taylour in Tholos Tomb IV at Pylos 
and with the four gold signet rings from the Griffin Warrior tomb. This situates
the Ring of Nestor within a longer history of scholarly engagement with elite 
signet rings as sources for religious imagery and sociopolitical symbolism. 
[10][6]\n\nModern summaries of the Griffin Warrior excavation emphasize the role
of its four gold signet rings, with their Minoan‑style iconography, in 
discussions of the transmission and adaptation of Minoan religious and 
mythological imagery into early Mycenaean elite contexts. These studies use 
comparative material, including earlier named rings such as the Ring of Nestor, 
to examine how specific seal designs were adopted, reinterpreted, and deployed 
in funerary and social settings over time. [2][6]\n\n## Provenance and 
collection\n\nPublished catalogues and digital library entries for Evans’s The 
ring of Nestor consistently describe the work as a study of a gold signet ring 
and associated seal material from a tomb at Thisbê in Boeotia, but they do not 
provide detailed, up‑to‑date information on the subsequent museum history or 
current collection location of the Ring of Nestor itself. The available records 
therefore document the ring’s archaeological provenance and its treatment in 
scholarship, while leaving the modern collection context less clearly specified 
in accessible summaries. [7][9]\n\n## References\n\n- Heidelberg University 
Library, \"The ring of Nestor. A glimpse into the Minoan after-world and a 
